From: Paul Whitmore <wit@fidalgo.Stanford.EDU>
Date: Thu, 10 Nov 94 01:30:35 PST
Subject: [#94-11-159] Camille Paglia as Educator

i just heard camille paglia harangue stanford, bastion of pc, and her
argument for reforming education was quite provocative. 

her modest proposal:
the world religions should be the basis of a true multicultural
curriculum. true understanding of art would introduce an appreciation of
the world's great weirdos. she mocked the claim that artists have all
been european white males who are part of some power elite- she used the
example of dante as a disenfranchised neurotic who spent his life
ejected from his culture, writing poetry putting all his enemies in hell.

understanding judeo-christian religion means seeing its most pungent
doctrines as relevant facts about the belief system. i am not sure when 
academics would be frank enough to cover the homophobic, misogynistic
elements of the bible, but the refusal to see how the judeo-christian
morality is anti-nature, anti-sex, etc. does not in any way diminish its
power and draw.

she repeatedly claimed that the right wing has accurately identified the
decadence that is rampant, and that true radicals need to
reconceptualize choices that offer real alternatives to the current fascist
drift.  as the avowed enemy of the pc who want to save the oppressed,
she claims to be  libertarian in all private matters, and a "clinton
democrat" in the public sphere.

since the point of thinking is to reach a level of excitement that spurs
action, i found her witty and energetic arguments vastly superior to
the lead balloons and timid mumbling that plague those who fear to offend.

she attacks women and ethnic studies programs as decredentializing
ghettos. Sex Studies should be the focal point, which means recognizing
the biology of human animals. through art, students could turn their
attention to the human body, which she thinks would catalyze a new
renaissance.  
 
most of her critique of the academy is directed toward the french theory
people, and so it may sound irrelevant to scientists. yet, perhaps most
undergrads now believe the deconstructionist's version of creationism,
that even biology is a socially constructed discipline. 

paglia argues for a spiritual experience through art. that's her famous 
pagan phallicism. she does not sharply connect this to the dynamic
power that would be found in a curriculum that included knowledge of the
workings of economic markets, ecosystems, and computer networks.

but she is at least aware of one deep threat to our freedom to think.
her vivid openness antagonizes the denial and fear that are rotting
intellectual discourse.

as a singular voice, she has a far greater respect for knowledge
and honesty than the people who see paternalistic conspiracies in
everything written before 1973. in her advocacy for the dyonisiac
energy of pagan sensibility, her enthusiasm for art and cleverness
exemplify the vitality of her perspective.


