Geoff Dale has the correct interpretation of the measure, as I understand
it. This measure does promote the concept of self-ownership, but, IMHO,
does not carry it far enough. Terminally ill patients still need to request
a lethal prescription from their doctor. This would seem to lend a 
dependent aspect to the process. 

Thomas Szasz has written that he does not favor physicians granting lethal 
procedures because they are supposed to be in the healing business. 
However, he is completely in favor of individuals having access to 
painless ways of ending their lives...if that is _their_ choice. I agree.

And isn't that what it's all about...free, personal choice? Even if 
individual extropians favor immortality, as libertarians shouldn't they 
honor others' free-choice decisions to discorporate if they so choose?


Onward!

